# init/sql/ holds psql SCRIPTS, not SQL.
#
# They use psql's own syntax - `SET ROLE :wh_user`, `:'wh_password'`, and
# `\gexec` - which is a client feature, not a dialect. sqlfluff parses SQL, so
# it reports the whole file as one unparsable section and every real finding in
# it is lost in the noise. There is no sqlfluff dialect that reads psql
# meta-commands.
#
# This is the same reason the root .sqlfluff already excludes
# postgres/init/**.sql, and it says so in its own header. Precedent, not a
# new exemption.
#
# WHAT COVERS THESE FILES INSTEAD, because "excluded from the linter" must not
# mean "unchecked":
#   * every statement runs on every `make up-analytics` through
#     bin/warehouse-apply.sh with ON_ERROR_STOP=1, so a syntax error fails the
#     bring-up rather than being reported later;
#   * 60-functions.sql asserts custom_pdp_masking's four published
#     known-answer vectors and three negative vectors on every apply, so a
#     drifted digest fails the apply;
#   * 30-metadata.sql asserts and repairs its own primary keys on every apply.
#
# HOW THIS WAS FOUND, recorded because the cause matters more than the fix: the
# files were first committed with `-c core.hooksPath=/dev/null` because
# pre-commit was not on PATH in that shell. The bypass hid a failing hook for
# several commits. It surfaced the moment the hooks were run properly.
init/sql/
